quals the\n\npolynomial Cat k(W;q)evaluated at q=ω d.\n\nThe k= 1 case of Theorem 3.7 was proven in a case-by-case manner by Bessis and Reiner [BR].\n\nKrattenthaler and M¨uller gave a case-by-case proof for arbitrary k. The above argument proves\n\nTheorem 3.7 uniformly modulo the Weak Conjecture.\n\n4. The Strong Conjecture\n\nIn this section we give evidence for the Strong Conjecture by computing certain subsets of the\n\nvariety V Θ and showing that they consist of multiplicity one points and have W×Z kh-equivariant\n\ninjections onto explicit subsets of Park NC\n\nW(k).\n\nTo state our evidence for the Strong Conjecture, we introduce a stratiﬁcation of V Θ which is\n\nstable under the W×Z kh-action. Deﬁne the dimension of a point v∈V Θ to be the minimum\n\ndimension dim(X) of a ﬂat X∈ L such that v∈X. Let V Θ(d) denote the set of d-dimensional\n\npoints in V Θ. Then we have a disjoint union decomposition\n\nV Θ=V Θ(0) ⊎V Θ(1) ⊎ · · · ⊎ V Θ(n),\nW(k)whose image is precisely\n\nthe set of k-W-noncrossing parking functions [w, X 1≤ · · · ≤ X k]with dim(X 1) = 1.\n\nWe remark that when n= 1, the sets V Θ(0) and V Θ(1) exhaust V Θ, so that Propositions 4.1\n\nand 4.4 reprove the Strong Conjecture in rank 1. Roughly speaking, Propositions 4.1 and 4.4 state\n\nthat the Strong Conjecture holds ‘in dimension ≤1’ for arbitrary W.\n\nProof. Without loss of generality, assume the underlying Coxeter element c∈W is bipartite and\n\nlet P⊆V be the associated Coxeter plane.\n\nFor any ﬂat X∈ L, there exists w∈W such that X=V w. Since the polynomial map Θ : V→V\n\nis W-equivariant, this implies that Θ(X)⊆X and the restriction Θ|X of Θ to X is a well deﬁned\n\npolynomial map of degree kh + 1.\n\nAssume that the ﬂat X∈ L is one-dimensional. Identifying the coordinate ring C[X] with C[x],\n\nup to scaling Θ|X can be identiﬁed with αx kh+1 −x for some α∈C×. The solutions to this equation\n\nall have multiplicity one, and are given by\n\n{0,β,ωβ,...,ω kh−1 β},\n\n\n8. Type A\n\nWe prove the Weak Conjecture in type A. The proof is not so diﬀerent from the k= 1 case\n\npresented in [ARR] and is a direct counting argument. As in the k= 1 case, a proof of the\n\nIntermediate Conjecture in type A has been hindered by the lack of a suﬃciently simple hsop Θ\n\nwhich would allow an explicit description of V Θ.\n\n8.1. The Main Conjecture in type A. Let W=S n have type A n−1, so that h=n and let\n\nk≥1. Fix a pair (w, g d)∈S n×Z kn with 0 ≤d < kn and a primitive kn th root of unity ω. Also\n\nﬁx the Coxeter element c= (1,2,...,n)∈S n.\n\nRecall that the Weak Conjecture asserts that the number of equivalence classes in Park NC\n\nS n(k)\n\nwhich are ﬁxed by (w, g d) equals (kn + 1)mult w(ω d), where mult w(ω d) is the multiplicity of the\n\neigenvalue ω d in the action of w on the reﬂection representation. The spectrum of w can be\n\ndetermined from its cycle decomposition, so that mult w(ω d) is the number of cycles of w, less one,\n\nwhen d= 0 and mult w(ω d) is the number of cycles of w of length divisible by m when d > 0, where\n\nm:= kn\n\ngcd(kn,d)≥2 is the order of g d. We consider the cases d= 0 and d > 0 separately.\n\n38 BRENDON RHOADES\n\nWhen d= 0, the number of ﬁxed points of (w, 1) equals (kh + 1)dim(V w)because of the S n-\n\nisomorphism between Park N C\n\nS n(k) and Park n(k) given in Section 1.\n\nWe therefore assume that d > 0 and let\n\nr m(w) := |{cycles of w of length divisible by m}|.\n\nWe aim to prove\n\n(8.1) (kn + 1)r m(w)=|Park NC\n\nW(k)(w,g d)|.\n\nThe idea behind proving Equation 8.1 is to show that both sides count the following set of objects.\n\nDeﬁnition 8.1. Let w∈S n and g d 6= 1 as above. Identify g with the permutation (1,2,...,kn)(0)\n\nacting on [kn]∪ {0}. Call a function f: [n]→[kn]∪ {0}(w, g d)-equivariant if\n\nf(w(j)) = g d f(j)\n\nfor every j∈[n].\n\nProving that (w, g d)-equivariant functions are counted by the right hand side of Equation 8.1 is\n\n\n44 BRENDON RHOADES\n\nCorollary 9.10. Suppose that the Weak Conjecture holds for W=W(Φ) and W is crystallo-\n\ngraphic. Then the number of k-W-noncrossing partitions equals the number of length k geometric\n\nmultichains of nonnesting ﬂats.\n\n10. Open problems\n\nThe most obvious open problem is to prove the Weak Conjecture for the exceptional reﬂection\n\ngroups EFH. As previously mentioned, this is a priori an inﬁnite problem since the Fuss parameter\n\nk could be any positive integer. More ambitiously, one could hope for a type-uniform proof of the\n\nstrong conjecture in all types.\n\nProblem 10.1. Give a uniform proof of the Strong or Intermediate Conjectures.\n\nIn light of Etingof’s result [ARR, Theorem 12.1], one could assume that the locus V Θ is reduced\n\nand still obtain a uniform proof of the Intermediate Conjecture.\n\nThe nonnesting parking space Park NN\n\nΦ(k) carries an action of W, but the author is unaware of\n\na natural action of the full cyclic group W×Z kh which gives Park NN\n\nΦ(k)∼\n\n=W×Z kh Park NC\n\nW(k). This\n\ninability to extend the action beyond W in the nonnesting case persists even in the k= 1 case\n\n[ARR].\n\nProblem 10.2. Deﬁne a W×Z kh-set structure on Park NN\n\nW(k)which gives Park NN\n\nΦ(k)∼\n\n=W×Z kh\n\nPark NC\n\nW(k).\n\nWhile this paper has focused exclusively on real reﬂection groups, there has the notions of\n\nCoxeter elements, absolute order, and noncrossing partitions make sense in the broader context of\n\nwell-generated complex reﬂection groups. It is natural to ask how much of the theory presented in\n\n[ARR] and this paper generalize to the complex setting.\n\nWriting Θ = (θ 1,...,θ n),\n\nwe can take a basis (x 1,...,x n) of V∗such that the linear map x i 7→ θ i is W-equivariant and\n\ndeﬁne Park alg\n\nW(k) to be C[V]/(Θ −x) as in the real case. The proof of [ARR, Proposition 2.11] goes\n\nthrough to show that Park alg\n\nW(k)∼\n\n=W×Z kh C[V]/(Θ). One can formulate the Strong and Intermediate\n\nConjectures in the same way.\n\nUnfortunately, the rational Che